mechanisms.how_it_works:
Two nested IIFEs. The OUTER one runs the guard before any real code:

```js
const PICKER_VERSION = 35;
const prev = window.__AI_PICKER_STATE__;
if (prev && prev.version === PICKER_VERSION && prev.isAlive()) return;
```

Three conditions must ALL hold to skip: a marker exists, its version matches this build, and it is still alive. The liveness test is registered at the very END of the inner IIFE (so a script that crashed during setup never claims to be alive):

```js
window.__AI_PICKER_STATE__ = {
  version: PICKER_VERSION,
  isAlive() { try { return !!chrome.runtime?.id; } catch (_) { return false; } }
};
```

`chrome.runtime.id` is the probe: in a live context it is the extension ID string; in an orphaned context the property access itself throws, caught by the try/catch, returning false. So an orphan reports dead, the guard falls through, and the fresh script re-runs and overwrites the marker — taking over the page.

The version field handles the second failure mode: a script that is genuinely alive but built from an OLD content.js (extension updated while a tab was open and Chrome kept the old script running under the old context). Version mismatch also falls through to re-injection.

The service worker mirrors this from the other side (background.js ~50–66) so it does not blind-inject on every action:

```js
const CONTENT_VERSION = 35;
async function ensureContentScript(tabId) {
  try {
    const r = await chrome.tabs.sendMessage(tabId, { type: "PING" });
    if (r?.v === CONTENT_VERSION) return true;   // live + current → done
  } catch (_) { /* no script, or orphan that cannot answer → fall through */ }
  try { await chrome.scripting.executeScript({ target: { tabId }, files: ["content.js"] }); return true; }
  catch (_) { return false; }
}
```

An orphan cannot answer PING (the message port belongs to the dead context), so the sendMessage rejects and injection proceeds. A live-but-stale script answers with the wrong `v` and is also re-injected. Together the two halves make injection idempotent AND self-healing.

A single host div, appended to `document.body || document.documentElement`, carries the positioning and the stacking context but has ZERO geometry:

```js
ui.root = document.createElement("div");
ui.root.id = "__ai-picker-host";
Object.assign(ui.root.style, {
  position: "fixed", top: "0", left: "0", width: "0", height: "0",
  margin: "0", padding: "0", border: "0",
  zIndex: String(Z), pointerEvents: "none"
});
ui.shadow = ui.root.attachShadow({ mode: "open" });
```

The 0×0 host is deliberate: it cannot ever affect page layout or hit-testing regardless of where in the document it lands, and its own inline styles are written with `Object.assign(el.style, …)` (inline = author-important-adjacent specificity) so page CSS cannot restyle it. The `pointer-events: none` on the host propagates to the whole subtree — the overlay is invisible to hit-testing, which is the primary reason `document.elementFromPoint` and event targeting still find the page's real elements underneath.

Everything visible lives inside the shadow root: one `<style>` element and seven divs (four box-model layers, HUD, hint bar, toast). Shadow encapsulation gives both directions of isolation for free — page selectors do not match shadow-internal nodes, and the overlay's `:host, * { box-sizing: border-box }` reset does not touch the page.

The z-index is a layered budget, not a single value:
```js
const Z = 2147483640;   // just below max (2147483647 = INT32_MAX)
// .layer  → Z
// .hud    → Z + 2
// .hint   → Z + 2
// ruler   → Z + 3   (canvas, in light DOM)
// .toast  → Z + 4   = 2147483644, still < INT32_MAX
```
Using exactly `2147483647` would forfeit the ability to stack the picker's own chrome — the HUD, hint bar and toast must sit ABOVE the highlight rectangles, and there is no room above the max. Reserving 7 units of headroom is the whole trick, and it still beats every practical page value (real-world offenders top out around 999999 / 2147483647 for the rare `z-index: 2147483647` cookie wall; the extension accepts losing to that one case in exchange for internal layering).

Note the layers are inside a shadow root but the host establishes the stacking context, so the internal z-indexes only order the picker's own children — they do not need to beat the page individually. The host's Z is what fights the page.

One `getBoundingClientRect()` (the border box, in viewport coordinates, transforms included) plus one `getComputedStyle()` read of the twelve edge widths. All four rectangles are then pure arithmetic outward/inward from that single rect:

```js
const r  = elm.getBoundingClientRect();
const cs = getComputedStyle(elm);
const n  = (v) => parseFloat(v) || 0;   // "12px" → 12, "auto"/"" → 0

const m  = { t: n(cs.marginTop),      r: n(cs.marginRight),      b: …, l: … };
const bd = { t: n(cs.borderTopWidth), r: n(cs.borderRightWidth), b: …, l: … };
const p  = { t: n(cs.paddingTop),     r: n(cs.paddingRight),     b: …, l: … };

// margin box  = border box grown by margins
place(ui.margin,  { top: r.top - m.t, left: r.left - m.l,
                    width: r.width + m.l + m.r, height: r.height + m.t + m.b });
// border box  = the rect itself, drawn as OUTLINE only (no fill)
place(ui.border,  { top: r.top, left: r.left, width: r.width, height: r.height });
// padding box = border box shrunk by border widths
place(ui.padding, { top: r.top + bd.t, left: r.left + bd.l,
                    width: r.width - bd.l - bd.r, height: r.height - bd.t - bd.b });
// content box = padding box shrunk by paddings
place(ui.content, { top: r.top + bd.t + p.t, left: r.left + bd.l + p.l,
                    width:  r.width  - bd.l - bd.r - p.l - p.r,
                    height: r.height - bd.t - bd.b - p.t - p.b });
```

Key details that make it work:

- **`place()` clamps negatives**: `width: Math.max(0, box.width)` — an element narrower than its own horizontal padding would otherwise produce a negative width and throw off the layer.
- **Viewport coordinates map 1:1**: every layer is `position: fixed` inside the shadow root, so `getBoundingClientRect()`'s viewport-relative numbers are used verbatim with NO scroll-offset arithmetic. This is the single biggest simplification in the whole overlay — it is why the code has no `window.scrollY` anywhere in the highlight path.
- **DevTools' exact colour semantics**, so the picture reads instantly to anyone who has used Chrome DevTools: margin `rgba(246,178,107,.30)` orange, padding `rgba(147,196,125,.30)` green, content `rgba(111,168,220,.30)` blue. Note the layers are painted in DOM order margin → padding → content → border, so the alphas STACK: the content region shows all three tints composited, which is why the shrinking rectangles read as nested rather than as flat patches.
- **Border layer is transparent fill + `outline: 2px solid #2563eb; outline-offset: -1px`** — an outline, not a border, so it adds no box of its own and `-1px` offset pulls the stroke fully inside the rect (an outline is drawn OUTSIDE the box by default; without the offset the highlight would be 2px larger than the element on every side and would misreport tight layouts).
- **Sub-pixel smoothing via CSS transition**: `.layer { transition: top .05s linear, left .05s linear, width .05s linear, height .05s linear; }` — a 50ms interpolation on the geometry means the highlight *glides* between elements as the cursor moves instead of teleporting. Cheap, and it is the entire reason the overlay feels like DevTools rather than like a debug rectangle.

`clearHighlight()` sets `display:none` on all four layers plus the HUD — it does not tear down the host, so re-highlighting is a style write with no allocation.

A single `mousemove` listener registered in the CAPTURE phase on `document`:

```js
const opts = { capture: true };
document.addEventListener("mousemove", onMouseMove, opts);

function onMouseMove(e) {
  const t = e.composedPath ? e.composedPath()[0] : e.target;
  if (!t || t.nodeType !== Node.ELEMENT_NODE) return;
  if (t === ui.root || ui.root?.contains(t)) return;   // never target ourselves
  target = t;
  highlight(t);
}
```

Three distinct decisions:

1. **Capture phase** — the listener runs on the way DOWN, before any page handler on any ancestor or on the target itself. A page that `stopPropagation()`s mousemove (drag libraries do this constantly) cannot blind the picker.
2. **`composedPath()[0]` instead of `e.target`** — this is the shadow-DOM fix. When the cursor is over a node inside an OPEN shadow root, event retargeting rewrites `e.target` to the shadow HOST, so `e.target` on a `<my-card>` with internal `<button>` reports `<my-card>` and the picker can never select the button. `composedPath()` returns the full un-retargeted path from the true innermost node up through shadow boundaries to `window`; index 0 is the actual deepest node under the cursor. For a CLOSED shadow root, composedPath is truncated at the host — you get the host, exactly what `e.target` would have given, so this is a graceful no-worse fallback rather than a solution.
3. **Non-element guard** — `composedPath()[0]` can be a text node or `document` in some paths; the nodeType check drops those without clearing the current highlight.

There is no rAF throttle: `highlight()` runs synchronously on every mousemove event, meaning one `getBoundingClientRect()` + one `getComputedStyle()` + twelve `parseFloat`s + four style writes per mouse event. It works because both reads happen before any of the writes in the same function, so there is exactly one forced layout per frame, not four.

Content is rebuilt from scratch on every highlight, using `textContent` on individual spans (never innerHTML with page data — page-controlled class names and IDs go into this label, so string interpolation would be an XSS-into-our-own-shadow-root):

```js
const tag  = elm.tagName.toLowerCase();
const id   = elm.id ? `#${elm.id}` : "";
const cls  = Array.from(elm.classList).slice(0, 3).map(c => `.${c}`).join("");
const more = elm.classList.length > 3 ? "…" : "";   // Tailwind pages have 20+ classes
const w = Math.round(rect.width), h = Math.round(rect.height);
// row: <span.tag>div</span> <span.id>#hero</span> <span.cls>.card.p-4…</span> <span.dim>320 × 180</span>
// second line: <span.sel>{buildSelector(elm)}</span>
```
`.dim` uses `margin-left: auto` inside a flex row so the dimensions always right-align regardless of how long the class list is, and the whole `.hud` is `white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; max-width: 460px` so a pathological class list truncates instead of exploding.

Positioning is measure-then-place, which requires the content to already be in the DOM and displayed:

```js
ui.hud.style.display = "block";                 // must be visible to measure
const hudRect = ui.hud.getBoundingClientRect();
let top = rect.top - hudRect.height - 8;        // prefer ABOVE the element
if (top < 4) top = rect.bottom + 8;             // no room → FLIP below
let left = rect.left;                           // left-align with the element
const maxLeft = window.innerWidth - hudRect.width - 6;
if (left > maxLeft) left = Math.max(6, maxLeft); // clamp right edge
if (left < 6) left = 6;                          // clamp left edge
```
The `Math.max(6, maxLeft)` inside the right-clamp is the non-obvious part: if the HUD is WIDER than the viewport, `maxLeft` goes negative, and without the max the HUD would be pushed off-screen to the left while trying to fit its right edge.

Four directions mapped onto the four DOM relationships, with an explicit bootstrap when nothing has been hovered yet:

```js
function navigate(direction) {
  if (!target) { target = document.body; highlight(target); return; }  // bootstrap
  let next = null;
  switch (direction) {
    case "up":    next = target.parentElement; break;
    case "down":  next = firstElementChildInViewport(target); break;
    case "left":  next = target.previousElementSibling; break;
    case "right": next = target.nextElementSibling; break;
  }
  if (next && next !== ui.root && next.nodeType === Node.ELEMENT_NODE) {
    target = next;
    target.scrollIntoView({ block: "nearest", inline: "nearest" });
    highlight(target);
  }
}
```

Details that matter:
- **`scrollIntoView({ block: "nearest", inline: "nearest" })`** — `nearest` is the correct choice and a common mistake: it scrolls only if the element is actually out of view, and only by the minimum amount. `center` or `start` would jerk the page on every keystroke even when the target is already visible.
- **`firstElementChildInViewport`** is misnamed — it does no viewport test at all; it returns the first element child that is not the overlay host:
  ```js
  for (const child of elm.children) { if (child === ui.root) continue; return child; }
  return null;
  ```
  The `continue` matters because the host is appended to `<body>`, so navigating DOWN from `<body>` would otherwise land on the overlay.
- **Null-safe by construction** — `parentElement`, `previousElementSibling`, `nextElementSibling` all return null at the boundaries and the `if (next && …)` simply does nothing, so the highlight sticks at the edges of the tree instead of clearing.
- **No shadow-boundary crossing** — `parentElement` returns null at a shadow root, so UP from a shadow-internal node dead-ends. The extension's own ancestor-context code elsewhere (content.js ~5120) does handle this with `el.parentElement || el.getRootNode?.()?.host`, but the picker's navigate() does not.

The keys arrive via two paths (see the lifecycle mechanism): directly from `onKeyDown` when the page has focus, or relayed as `NAV_KEY` messages when the side panel holds focus.

One reusable node in the shadow root, `position: fixed; bottom: 24px; right: 24px; z-index: Z+4` (the highest layer in the whole overlay — a toast must sit above the HUD and the ruler canvas), styled with `opacity: 0; transform: translateY(10px); transition: opacity .2s, transform .2s` and a `.show` class that sets `opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0)`.

The replay trick:
```js
clearTimeout(toastTimer);
ui.toast.className = `toast${isError ? " error" : ""}`;   // resets — DROPS .show
ui.toast.innerHTML = "";                                   // rebuild content
ui.toast.append(ico, txt);
requestAnimationFrame(() => ui.toast.classList.add("show"));
toastTimer = setTimeout(() => ui.toast?.classList.remove("show"), 3000);
```
Assigning `className` wholesale removes `.show`, snapping the node back to the hidden state; adding `.show` back in the SAME task would produce no transition because the browser coalesces both style changes into one computed value. Deferring to the next animation frame forces a style recalculation between the two states, so the transition actually runs. This is why it is `requestAnimationFrame` and not a bare call.

Content is `{ icon, title, subtitle }` — icon is `✓` or `⚠`, colour-coded via the `.error` class (red background/border/text). The subtitle carries the payload-specific detail: on success the generated selector, on failure a diagnostic (`"تحقق من أذونات الحافظة لهذه الصفحة"` — check clipboard permissions for this page). It is built with `textContent` on child nodes, never innerHTML with page data.

`showToast` lazily calls `buildUI()` if the overlay does not exist, so it can be used outside pick mode.

**The picker does not handle iframes at all — this is a gap, not a mechanism, and it is worth reporting precisely so the port does not inherit it.**

The manifest's `content_scripts` entry for content.js declares no `all_frames`, so it defaults to `false` → top frame only. The service worker's re-injection is `chrome.scripting.executeScript({ target: { tabId }, files: ["content.js"] })` with no `allFrames: true` → top frame only. Consequence: hovering over iframe content highlights the `<iframe>` ELEMENT (a single opaque rectangle), and clicking picks the iframe element itself. There is no per-frame overlay, no coordinate translation from child-frame viewport space to parent viewport space, and no cross-frame message routing.

Iframes ARE handled in a completely different subsystem — the extraction/export walker, which descends into same-origin frames:
```js
function deepAll(root, out = [], depth = 0) {
  if (out.length >= 9000 || depth > 14) return out;   // hard budget caps
  for (const n of root.querySelectorAll("*")) {
    if (ui.root && (n === ui.root || ui.root.contains(n))) continue;
    out.push(n);
    const sr = shadowRootOf(n);                        // open OR closed
    if (sr) deepAll(sr, out, depth + 1);
    if (n.tagName === "IFRAME" || n.tagName === "FRAME") {
      try { const doc = n.contentDocument; if (doc?.documentElement) deepAll(doc.documentElement, out, depth + 1); }
      catch (_) { /* cross-origin — unreachable by design */ }
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```
Note `shadowRootOf`, which is the one place the extension used an API with no web equivalent:
```js
function shadowRootOf(el) {
  try { if (chrome.dom?.openOrClosedShadowRoot) return chrome.dom.openOrClosedShadowRoot(el); } catch (_) {}
  return el.shadowRoot || null;   // fallback: open roots only
}
```
`chrome.dom.openOrClosedShadowRoot` is extension-only, requires no permission, and returns a CLOSED shadow root — invisible to every bookmarklet and userscript. The `depth > 14` and `out.length >= 9000` caps exist because shadow+iframe recursion on a real app is unbounded otherwise.

`pick(elm)` does five things in order: stores `lastPicked` (a module-level ref that deliberately SURVIVES deactivation, so the side panel can later walk to the parent via `INSPECT_ANCESTOR` after the picker is off), builds the data object via `collect()`, formats it, copies it, and broadcasts `{type:"PICK_RESULT", data, payload}` so the side panel updates.

`collect(elm)` assembles, gated by settings: `tag`, `selector` (buildSelector), `text` (textContent, whitespace-collapsed, 200 chars), `metrics`, `outerHTML` (sanitised clone), `id`, `classes` (ALL of them, unlike the HUD's 3), optional `xpath`, optional approximate `tailwind`, `styleInfo`, `attributes`, `accessibility`, optional `computedStyles` (a fixed 25-property KEY_STYLES subset — display/position/box-sizing/width/height/margin/padding/border/border-radius/color/background-color/font-*/line-height/text-align/flex-*/justify-content/align-items/gap/grid-template-columns/z-index/opacity/overflow).

`getMetrics` returns BOTH coordinate spaces, which is the useful detail: `{width, height, x: r.x + scrollX, y: r.y + scrollY, viewportX: r.x, viewportY: r.y}` — document coords for reproducibility, viewport coords for screenshotting.

`cleanOuterHTML` clones then `sanitize`s: **every `on*` attribute is stripped unconditionally** ("previews render this markup, and nothing from a scanned page should be able to run"), `style` is stripped unless `verbatim`, and class lists longer than 8 are truncated with `…` unless `verbatim`. The `verbatim` flag exists because the readable payload trims for legibility but a component that will be *pasted somewhere and expected to work* dies if you drop its ninth utility class or its inline styles. Output is capped at 6000 chars with a `<!-- … truncated … -->` marker.

Clipboard write is two-tier:
```js
try { await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text); return true; }
catch (_) {
  const ta = document.createElement("textarea");
  ta.value = text;
  Object.assign(ta.style, { position: "fixed", top: "0", left: "0", opacity: "0" });
  document.body.appendChild(ta); ta.focus(); ta.select();
  const ok = document.execCommand("copy");
  ta.remove(); return ok;
}
```
The fallback matters: `navigator.clipboard` requires a secure context and a transient user activation, and fails on `http://` pages and inside some sandboxed contexts. `position: fixed; opacity: 0` (rather than `display:none` or off-screen) is required — a hidden or unrendered textarea cannot be selected, and off-screen positioning causes a scroll jump.

Capture the whole visible viewport at the browser level, then crop client-side using the element's own rect, with device-pixel-ratio scaling:

1. `elm.scrollIntoView({ block: "center", behavior: "instant" })` — the capture is viewport-only, so the element must be in view; `center` (not `nearest`) is right here because a partially-visible element would be clipped.
2. **`clearHighlight()` then `await sleep(120)`** — the crucial step. The overlay is drawn in the page and WOULD appear in the capture; hiding it and waiting ~2 frames (the 120ms also covers the 50ms layer transition finishing) yields a clean shot. The highlight is restored immediately after the capture returns.
3. `chrome.runtime.sendMessage({type:"CAPTURE_TAB"})` → service worker `chrome.tabs.captureVisibleTab(windowId, {format:"png"})` → data URL back.
4. Crop with DPR compensation — the capture is in DEVICE pixels, the rect is in CSS pixels:
```js
const dpr = window.devicePixelRatio || 1;
const r = elm.getBoundingClientRect();
canvas.width  = Math.max(1, Math.round(r.width  * dpr));
canvas.height = Math.max(1, Math.round(r.height * dpr));
ctx.drawImage(img, r.left*dpr, r.top*dpr, r.width*dpr, r.height*dpr,
                   0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
```
The 9-argument `drawImage(src, sx,sy,sw,sh, dx,dy,dw,dh)` does the crop in one call. `Math.max(1, …)` prevents a zero-dimension canvas throwing.
5. `canvas.toDataURL("image/png")` → synthetic `<a download>` click.

A different overlay strategy from the picker's, and instructive by contrast: a single `<canvas>` in the LIGHT DOM at `position:fixed; top:0; left:0; z-index: Z+3; pointer-events:none`, sized to the viewport in device pixels with a CSS size in CSS pixels — the standard HiDPI pattern:
```js
const dpr = window.devicePixelRatio || 1;
canvas.width  = innerWidth  * dpr;  canvas.style.width  = innerWidth  + "px";
canvas.height = innerHeight * dpr;  canvas.style.height = innerHeight + "px";
// then in the draw loop: ctx.scale(dpr, dpr) inside save/restore, so all
// drawing code speaks CSS pixels and stays crisp.
```
Drawing runs on a continuous `requestAnimationFrame` loop (not on mousemove), and the mouse handlers only mutate `{sx, sy, ex, ey, dragging}` — decoupling input rate from paint rate. Each frame: clear, draw a dashed crosshair (`setLineDash([4,4])`) at the current pointer spanning the full viewport, and if dragging, fill+stroke the drag rectangle and draw a rounded label `"{w} × {h} px"` whose position flips (`if (lx + tw > innerWidth) lx = ex - tw - 12; if (ly > innerHeight - 10) ly = ey - 12`) using the same flip-and-clamp logic as the HUD.

The tool shares `injectCursor()`/`removeCursor()` and the `swallow()` event killer with the picker, and is made mutually exclusive with it (`if (active) deactivate();` at the top of `startRuler`) because a single nullable `ui.cursorStyle` cannot have two owners. `stopRuler` cancels the rAF, removes the canvas, and posts `RULER_STOPPED`.

The comment is explicit that this was measured, not assumed. What the panel CAN observe is focus moving into the frame, which is the same gesture seen from outside. Each frame carries `frame.dataset.idx = box.dataset.idx` — the index of the component it shows. Then:
```js
window.addEventListener("blur", () => {
  const el = document.activeElement;
  if (!el || !el.classList?.contains("ui-preview__frame")) return;
  const c = shownComponents()[Number(el.dataset.idx)];
  if (c) openUiSheet(c); else el.blur();
});
```
When the window blurs, if `document.activeElement` is one of our preview frames, the user just clicked that frame. The second half is the subtle part: focus must GENUINELY leave the frame afterwards, or every subsequent switch away from the window looks like another click and reopens the sheet. Calling `.blur()` on the iframe does NOT achieve this — `openUiSheet` ends with `$("uiSheetClose").focus()`, and moving focus to a real element is what does. `closeUiSheet` sets `hidden` and clears `uiSheetStage.innerHTML` — otherwise the frame keeps animating off-screen forever. Escape closes it via a document-level keydown guarded on `!$("uiSheet").hidden`. The card ALSO has a normal `preview.addEventListener('click', …)` for the region of the preview box not covered by the frame (and for the badges layer, which is `pointer-events:none`).
